+/// Park the USB thread and wait for it to acknowledge. Only the main thread
+/// calls this, so the handshake needs no reentrancy.
+///
+/// The first loop is what makes back-to-back commands survivable. usbResume
+/// only clears the request flag, so a command issued within the USB thread's
+/// 0.2ms parking sleep finds usb_paused still set from the previous pause,
+/// takes that stale ack, and re-parks the thread before it ever reached
+/// tracker.poll(). The USB thread is the only reader of the IN endpoint, so a
+/// pipelined client starves it completely and every response goes unread until
+/// the routing table overflows. Measured: 4000 pipelined get_display_area
+/// yielded 0 routed responses and 3935 "pending table full" warnings. Waiting
+/// for a poll to complete since the last resume both guarantees the reader a
+/// turn and guarantees usb_paused was cleared, so the ack below is fresh.
+///
+/// Each wait is budgeted at 5000 x 0.2ms = 1s, against a worst-case poll() of
+/// ~101ms (a 100ms blocking recv, then a 1ms tryRecv that finds nothing and
+/// ends the drain loop), so exhausting a budget means the USB thread is wedged,
+/// not merely busy. Exhausting one is not fatal: the command proceeds
+/// unsynchronised, which is exactly the pre-patch behaviour, and usbResume
+/// still clears the flag. Nothing the USB thread does waits on the main thread,
+/// so there is no cycle to deadlock on.
+///
+/// Both loops also abandon the wait once the USB thread has exited. On SIGTERM
+/// that thread leaves first while the main thread is still draining a queued
+/// command batch, and without the usb_running test each of those commands burns
+/// both budgets: 300 queued commands turned a shutdown into ten minutes.
+fn usbPause() void {
+    var waits: u32 = 0;
+    while (usb_running.load(.acquire) and
+        usb_polls.load(.acquire) == usb_polls_at_resume and waits < 5000) : (waits += 1)
+    {
+        std.Thread.sleep(200_000);
+    }
+    if (waits >= 5000) log.err("USB thread made no progress since last resume", .{});
+
+    usb_pause_req.store(true, .release);
+    var spins: u32 = 0;
+    while (usb_running.load(.acquire) and
+        !usb_paused.load(.acquire) and spins < 5000) : (spins += 1)
+    {
+        std.Thread.sleep(200_000);
+    }
+    if (spins >= 5000) log.err("USB thread failed to pause", .{});
+}
+
+fn usbResume() void {
+    usb_polls_at_resume = usb_polls.load(.acquire);
+    usb_pause_req.store(false, .release);
+}
